1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,448
Last time we built Alder's pile coin by coin,
and read its p-value from the tail: 3.5%.

2
00:00:07,488 --> 00:00:09,971
Exact, and a fair amount of work.

3
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:15,159
In practice, people often skip the pile.
Three shortcuts are in everyday use:

4
00:00:15,199 --> 00:00:18,727
a bell curve, a ruler,
and brute-force flipping.

5
00:00:18,867 --> 00:00:24,404
Each gives Alder a p-value of its own.
Will all three land on the same side of 5%?

6
00:00:24,444 --> 00:00:28,604
And is there a clinic where
a shortcut goes badly wrong?

7
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:32,421
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

8
00:00:32,461 --> 00:00:34,433
Today: three shortcuts.

9
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:39,925
The first shortcut replaces
the pile with a bell curve of

10
00:00:39,965 --> 00:00:42,433
the same centre and the same spread.

11
00:00:42,473 --> 00:00:48,775
For Alder, that is centred on 8.02,
with a standard deviation of 2.22.

12
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:57,457
Alder's 13 is 4.98 above the centre. Divide
by the spread: 2.24 standard deviations out.

13
00:00:57,497 --> 00:01:01,389
The bell curve's two tails
beyond that hold 2.5%.

14
00:01:01,533 --> 00:01:07,210
That is the score test. It needs just three
numbers: observed, expected, and the spread.

15
00:01:07,250 --> 00:01:12,528
It is our old friend, observed minus
expected, measured in standard units.

16
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:18,689
The second shortcut never looks at the
count. It goes back to the sliders.

17
00:01:18,729 --> 00:01:22,661
Alder's gamma sits 0.87 above the reference,

18
00:01:22,701 --> 00:01:27,293
and Episode 9 gave it a
standard error of 0.43.

19
00:01:27,433 --> 00:01:38,706
0.87 over 0.43 is 2.02 standard errors, and
a p-value of 4.4%. That is the Wald test.

20
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:44,590
The third shortcut takes last
episode's opening idea seriously.

21
00:01:44,630 --> 00:01:49,241
Flip Alder's thirty coins ten
thousand times, and simply count.

22
00:01:49,281 --> 00:01:52,789
This run gives a p-value of 3.8%.

23
00:01:52,933 --> 00:01:56,291
It homes in on the exact
pile as the rounds add up.

24
00:01:56,331 --> 00:02:02,408
But with ten thousand rounds, it cannot report
anything rarer than one in ten thousand.

25
00:02:02,448 --> 00:02:04,931
The software calls this one a bootstrap.

26
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:13,472
So, Alder four ways: 3.5%
exact, 2.5% by the bell curve,

27
00:02:13,512 --> 00:02:18,300
4.4% by the ruler, 3.8% by flipping.

28
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:24,087
All below 5%. All four flag Alder, and
none of the tests flags anyone else.

29
00:02:24,233 --> 00:02:26,906
But look at Elm. Exact and bell curve agree:

30
00:02:26,946 --> 00:02:30,678
about one and a half standard
units below the reference.

31
00:02:30,718 --> 00:02:36,700
The ruler says only 0.7. It is measuring
from a gamma that does not exist.

32
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:44,020
That is the ruler's first weakness. Its second
is one we have met: which standard error?

33
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:49,883
Record the risk factor sixty-five units
higher, as an age in years might be,

34
00:02:49,923 --> 00:02:56,991
and the textbook standard error of Alder's
gamma becomes 38. The test collapses to 0.02.

35
00:02:57,031 --> 00:03:01,370
Taken at the average case
mix, it stays at 2.02.

36
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:04,233
The bell curve's weakness is small numbers.

37
00:03:04,273 --> 00:03:06,812
Elm expects fewer than two readmissions,

38
00:03:06,852 --> 00:03:10,362
and a bell curve centred
there spills below zero.

39
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:15,392
In our crowds where every
provider equals the reference,

40
00:03:15,432 --> 00:03:19,180
among those with fewer than fifty patients,

41
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:25,911
the exact test flags 4.7%, the bell
curve 4.9%, and the ruler only 4.0%.

42
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:35,533
Each test also turns inside out
into an interval. For Alder's ratio:

43
00:03:35,573 --> 00:03:45,301
1.04 to 2.26, exact. 1.07 to 2.25 by the
bell curve. 1.02 to 2.31 by the ruler.

44
00:03:45,433 --> 00:03:50,577
So which one, when? The exact test for
flags: it is the software's default.

45
00:03:50,617 --> 00:03:52,907
The bell curve when you need a formula.

46
00:03:52,947 --> 00:03:57,169
It costs almost nothing, and next
time it will draw smooth limits.

47
00:03:57,209 --> 00:04:02,978
The ruler for anything built on gamma itself.
And flipping, as a check on the others.

48
00:04:04,567 --> 00:04:06,063
Let's gather it up.

49
00:04:06,103 --> 00:04:11,055
The bell curve swaps the pile for a normal
curve with the same centre and spread.

50
00:04:11,095 --> 00:04:15,044
The ruler measures the gap between
sliders in standard errors.

51
00:04:15,084 --> 00:04:18,486
Flipping rebuilds the pile
by brute force. On Alder,

52
00:04:18,526 --> 00:04:23,776
all three agree with the exact test.
On small clinics, they can part ways.

53
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:28,283
We now have a verdict for every
clinic. Five verdicts are easy to read.

54
00:04:28,323 --> 00:04:31,220
What about two hundred, or seven thousand?

55
00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:33,231
Next time: the funnel.

56
00:04:33,367 --> 00:04:37,472
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
